        <!-- Section 4: Total Views vs Qualified Views -->
        <section class="editorial-block">
          <h2>Total Views vs. Qualified Views: Why You Don't Get Paid for Every Play</h2>
          <p>
            The single most common misunderstanding among new TikTok creators is assuming that every view displayed on their public profile translates into money. When your video counter reads 1,000,000 views, TikTok's payment algorithm does not multiply 1,000,000 by your RPM. Instead, it filters out all non-qualifying views.
          </p>
          <p>
            According to TikTok's official Creator Rewards documentation, a view is monetized only when it satisfies all of the following conditions:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Minimum 5-Second Watch Time:</strong> The viewer must watch at least 5 continuous seconds of the video. If a user swipes away after 2 or 3 seconds, that view is counted toward your public analytics counter, but pays $0.00.</li>
            <li><strong>Origination from the 'For You' Feed:</strong> The view must originate from algorithmic recommendation on the For You page (FYP). Views coming from personal profile browsing, search result clicks, embedded links on external websites, or direct video sharing typically do not qualify for direct Creator Rewards payouts.</li>
            <li><strong>Unique Viewer Rule:</strong> Views are counted per unique user within a 24-hour period. If an enthusiastic fan watches your video four times in a row, only the first view is counted as a qualified monetized playback.</li>
            <li><strong>Video Length Mandate:</strong> The video must be at least 1 minute (60 seconds) in duration. Videos that are 58 or 59 seconds long receive zero monetization through Creator Rewards, regardless of how many millions of views they register.</li>
            <li><strong>Original Content Verification:</strong> Videos flagged for containing copyrighted commercial music without licensing, or identified as duplicate footage from movies, TV broadcasts, or other creators, are stripped of monetization eligibility.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For healthy accounts creating high-retention content, approximately <strong>50% to 70% of total views</strong> successfully qualify. If your content suffers from weak opening hooks or high immediate drop-offs, your qualification rate might dip below 30%, noticeably depressing your take-home revenue.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 5: Key Factors Affecting Your TikTok RPM -->
        <section class="editorial-block">
          <h2>Key Factors That Determine Your Personal TikTok RPM</h2>
          <p>
            Why does one creator earn $0.35 per 1,000 qualified views while another creator in a different category earns $1.85? TikTok's monetization system is auction-driven; advertisers bid to place their campaigns in front of specific audience segments. The following four factors exert the greatest influence on your channel's RPM:
          </p>
          
          <h3>1. Viewer Geographic Location (Audience Demographics)</h3>
          <p>
            Advertisers pay significantly higher CPMs to reach consumers with high disposable income. Audiences in Tier-1 countries—specifically the United States, United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, and parts of Western Europe—command the highest advertising bids. If 80% of your viewership resides in the United States, your RPM will likely hover between $0.80 and $1.50+. Conversely, if your audience is primarily concentrated in developing markets where digital ad inventory is cheaper, your RPM may average between $0.15 and $0.40.
          </p>

          <h3>2. Content Niche and Commercial Intent</h3>
          <p>
            Not all video topics carry equal commercial value. Niches where advertisers sell high-ticket products or high-margin services—such as personal finance, B2B software, investing, real estate, cryptocurrency, digital marketing, and luxury health—yield substantially higher RPMs. Brands in these categories are willing to pay top dollar to reach prospective buyers. In contrast, broad entertainment, viral meme accounts, gaming compilations, and lip-sync videos attract lower advertising bids because viewer purchase intent is diffuse.
          </p>

          <h3>3. Average Watch Time and Video Completion Rate</h3>
          <p>
            TikTok's ranking and monetization algorithms heavily penalize content that fails to retain viewer interest. A 2-minute video with a 65% completion rate demonstrates exceptional audience satisfaction. TikTok rewards this performance not only with wider algorithmic distribution on the For You page, but also with premium ad placement, which elevates your effective RPM. Developing strong 3-second opening hooks, eliminating dead air, and structuring your storytelling to keep viewers glued to the screen are essential strategies for boosting your RPM.
          </p>

          <h3>4. Search Intent and Search Query Alignment</h3>
          <p>
            TikTok has rapidly evolved into a primary search engine for Gen Z and millennial consumers. Videos that rank for specific, high-intent search terms (e.g., "best budgeting apps 2026" or "how to edit reels in Premiere Pro") attract targeted, contextual advertising. TikTok's Creator Rewards algorithm awards higher payout weightings to videos that satisfy user search intent, often pushing RPMs well above baseline entertainment rates.
          </p>
        </section>

